    "question": {
      ":question/id": "2b042f56-ccde-51ea-9511-6e2bf65b2181",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Simplify.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sin\\left(44^\\circ\\right)\\cot\\left(22^\\circ\\right)-1}{\\cos\\left(22^\\circ\\right)+\\sin\\left(22^\\circ\\right)}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Write $s=\\sin\\left(22^\\circ\\right)$ and $c=\\cos\\left(22^\\circ\\right)$. The **double-angle formula** gives $\\sin\\left(44^\\circ\\right)=2sc$.\n\n Also, $\\cot\\left(22^\\circ\\right)=\\frac{c}{s}$.\n\nRewrite the quotient ratio and use $s^2+c^2=1$ to obtain a difference of squares. Factor before cancelling.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{2sc\\frac{c}{s}-1}{c+s} &=\\frac{2c^2-1}{c+s} \\\\ &=\\frac{c^2-s^2}{c+s} \\\\ &=\\frac{(c-s)(c+s)}{c+s} \\\\ &=c-s \\\\ &=\\cos\\left(22^\\circ\\right)-\\sin\\left(22^\\circ\\right)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll cancellations use nonzero factors in the original expression.",
